\chapter{Interactive tutorials: the \texttt{tutorial} command}
\label{sec:tutorial-command}

This manual is a reference: it explains every command and why it works
the way it does.
A newcomer needs something else first, namely a guided first hour at a
real terminal, against their own Canvas, where each command is typed,
run and checked before the next one is introduced.
That is a different genre of text, and it needs a runner rather than a
chapter: something that opens a shell, records what the learner did,
tells them whether the step succeeded, and remembers where they were
when they come back the next day.

The [[tutorial]] command provides that runner.
[[canvaslms tutorial list]] shows the available tutorials and how far
the user has come in each; [[canvaslms tutorial run <id>]] starts or
resumes one; [[canvaslms tutorial review <id>]] shows the recorded
transcripts afterwards.
The tutorials themselves are chapters of their own
(\cref{tutorial-getting-started} and onwards); this chapter is about
the command that runs them.

\section{Why we embed pytorial}

What a tutorial runner must do has nothing to do with Canvas: open a
shell in a scratch directory so the learner types real commands, record
the transcript, validate it against patterns the author wrote, persist
progress so a tutorial can be resumed, and replay the record on request.
The [[pytorial]] package\footnote{%
  \url{https://github.com/dbosk/pytorial}} does exactly that, it is
written by the same author with the same literate toolchain as this
program, and it exposes [[add_argparse_subcommand]] for the purpose of
being mounted as a [[tutorial]] subcommand inside an [[argparse]]
application.
Writing our own runner would duplicate a few thousand lines of code
that we would then have to keep correct across terminals and
platforms; embedding costs us one dependency.

What we pay for that is worth stating.
Importing [[pytorial.cli]] imports [[typer]], which every invocation of
[[canvaslms]] now pays for, since every subcommand's [[add_command]]
runs before the command line is parsed.
The summary that pytorial prints after a completed run says
[[tutorial review <id>]] rather than [[canvaslms tutorial review <id>]].
And the first tutorial in the list, [[using-tutorials]], is pytorial's
own and not ours to edit; \cref{sec:tutorial-offered} returns to that.

\section{Why the tutorials are literate}

A tutorial is a teaching artefact, and the reasoning behind it, which
step comes first, what varies between two consecutive steps, why a
pattern validates the typed command rather than the output, is exactly
what a maintainer needs when the command-line interface changes and a
step must follow.
That reasoning is what noweb exists to keep next to the artefact.
Each tutorial is therefore a [[.nw]] file in [[src/canvaslms/tutorials]]
that tangles to the Markdown file pytorial loads and weaves to a chapter
of this manual, so the tutorial can be reviewed as prose and the reader
of the manual sees both the lesson and its design.

\section{Module outline}

The module is small: it locates the tutorials and the saved progress,
mounts pytorial's subcommand tree, and adapts pytorial's calling
convention to ours.

<<[[tutorial.py]]>>=
"""Interactive tutorials for canvaslms, powered by pytorial."""

import pathlib
import sys

from canvaslms import dirs
import pytorial.catalog
import pytorial.cli

<<functions>>

def add_command(subp):
  """Adds the tutorial command to argparse subparser subp"""
  add_tutorial_command(subp)
