\section{Where progress lives}

Pytorial records every run, its transcripts and its scratch workspaces
under a state directory.
Left to its own devices it would use a directory named after pytorial,
but this program keeps every file it writes under the directories that
the shared [[dirs]] object provides (\cref{sec:shared-appdirs}), so
that a user who looks for [[canvaslms]] state finds all of it in one
place.
We therefore hand pytorial a subdirectory of our own state directory.

<<functions>>=
def tutorial_state_dir():
  """Returns where tutorial progress and workspaces are stored"""
  return pathlib.Path(dirs.user_state_dir) / "tutorials"
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_progress_is_stored_with_the_other_canvaslms_state():
  """Tutorial state lives under the shared canvaslms state directory"""
  state_dir = tutorial.tutorial_state_dir()

  assert state_dir.is_relative_to(pathlib.Path(canvaslms.dirs.user_state_dir))
@

Two consequences of that record are worth knowing.
Transcripts are stored verbatim, so a learner should not paste an
access token into a tutorial shell; the [[getting-started]] tutorial
avoids asking them to for that reason too.
And a run remembers a digest of the tutorial it started from, so when a
release changes a bundled tutorial, a run in progress can no longer be
resumed and must be restarted with [[--restart]].
Editing a tutorial is thus a user-visible change.

\section{What the command must not require}
\label{sec:tutorial-no-canvas}

Every command function takes a [[canvas]] object
(\cref{command-line-interface}), and the main program creates that
object, reading the stored credentials, before it calls the function.
The tutorials are where a user learns to log in, so this command must
work before there are credentials: creating the Canvas object first
would abort with an error about the missing login, which is precisely
the lesson the tutorial was going to teach.
The command is therefore listed among those that need no Canvas object,
next to [[login]] itself, and receives [[None]] for [[canvas]].
The commands the learner types inside a tutorial shell are separate
processes that create their own Canvas objects, so nothing is lost.

\section{The tutorial shell}

A shell step opens [[bash]] in a scratch directory of its own, without
the user's profile or rc file, so that transcripts look the same on
every machine and no alias can stand in for a command.
The shell inherits the environment of the [[canvaslms]] process, which
is what makes [[canvaslms]] itself reachable inside it: with a
[[pipx]] or [[pip --user]] installation, an activated virtual
environment or [[poetry run]], the command is on the [[PATH]] the shell
inherits.
The one setup where it is not is a virtual environment that was never
activated, with [[canvaslms]] invoked by its full path; a learner in
that situation has to activate the environment first.

Shell steps need a POSIX terminal.
Pytorial handles the platforms itself: on Windows without WSL, the
help says so, [[tutorial list]] marks the affected tutorials, and
[[tutorial run]] refuses them before creating a run.
There is nothing for this module to do, and that is a reason to hand
pytorial its own shell runner rather than a wrapper of ours, since the
early refusal applies only to the runner pytorial recognises.

\section{The trust model}

A tutorial step may carry [[pre_command]], [[check_command]] and
[[post_command]], shell code written by the tutorial's author that
pytorial runs on the learner's behalf.
The standalone [[tutorial]] command refuses such code unless the user
passes [[--allow-shell]], because it loads whatever the user installed.
An embedded host mounts only the tutorials it chose, so pytorial
defaults to allowing the hooks there, and we keep that default.
The [[run]] verb still accepts [[--tutorial-path]], though, so a user
who points it at a tutorial they downloaded should pass
[[--no-allow-shell]] unless they trust its author.

Our own tutorials use none of the three hooks.
They validate typed commands and answers only, so they run unchanged
under [[--no-allow-shell]] and never execute anything the learner did
not type; \cref{sec:tutorial-contract} makes that a tested property.

\section{Registering the subcommand}

Pytorial builds the [[tutorial]] parser with its [[list]], [[run]] and
[[review]] verbs, and stores a callable in the [[tutorial_handler]]
attribute of the parsed arguments.
Our main program dispatches on [[func]] instead, so after mounting the
tree we set [[func]] on the parser pytorial returns.
Argparse copies a parser's defaults into the namespace before it hands
over to a subparser, so the attribute reaches the namespace whichever
verb was chosen.
Choosing no verb is an error that argparse reports itself, since
pytorial marks its verbs as required, and never reaches [[main]].

The keyword arguments exist for the tests, which must not touch the
user's real state directory; [[add_command]] itself passes none.

<<functions>>=
def add_tutorial_command(subp, *, state_root=None, tutorial_paths=None):
  """Adds the tutorial command and its verbs to argparse subparser subp

  `state_root` is where progress is stored, by default the canvaslms
  state directory; `tutorial_paths` are the tutorials to offer, by
  default the bundled ones.
  """
  if state_root is None:
    state_root = tutorial_state_dir()
  if tutorial_paths is None:
    tutorial_paths = offered_tutorial_paths()

  tutorial_parser = pytorial.cli.add_argparse_subcommand(
    subp,
    name="tutorial",
    namespace="canvaslms",
    state_root=state_root,
    tutorial_paths=tuple(tutorial_paths))
  tutorial_parser.set_defaults(func=tutorial_command)
  return tutorial_parser
@

The tests mount the command on a parser tree shaped like the one
[[main]] builds, so that what they parse is what the user's shell would
parse.

<<test helpers>>=
def build_parser(**kwargs):
  """Builds a parser tree holding only the tutorial command"""
  argp = argparse.ArgumentParser()
  subp = argp.add_subparsers(title="commands", dest="command",
                             required=True)
  tutorial.add_tutorial_command(subp, **kwargs)
  return argp
@

Each verb must parse and carry pytorial's handler, the verb must be
required, and [[func]] must be ours on every verb.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("verb", [
  ["list"],
  ["run", "getting-started"],
  ["review", "getting-started"],
])
def test_every_verb_carries_a_handler_and_our_func(verb):
  """Each tutorial verb dispatches through pytorial and through main()"""
  args = build_parser().parse_args(["tutorial"] + verb)

  assert callable(args.tutorial_handler)
  assert args.func is tutorial.tutorial_command

def test_the_verb_is_required():
  """canvaslms tutorial without a verb is rejected by argparse"""
  with pytest.raises(SystemExit):
    build_parser().parse_args(["tutorial"])
@

\section{Running the selected verb}

Pytorial's handlers return an exit status instead of exiting, so that
a host decides what to do with it.
We follow the rest of this program: a failure exits with that status,
so scripts and shells see it, and success returns quietly.
The [[config]] and [[canvas]] arguments are part of the command
protocol rather than of this command's work; [[canvas]] is [[None]] by
\cref{sec:tutorial-no-canvas}.

<<functions>>=
def tutorial_command(config, canvas, args):
  """Runs the tutorial verb selected on the command line"""
  status = pytorial.cli.run_argparse_command(args)
  if status:
    sys.exit(status)
@

Listing is the one verb that is cheap to run end to end: with a fresh
state directory it prints every offered tutorial as a tab-separated
row, since the console is not a terminal under the test runner.
A failing handler must turn into an exit with the same status, and a
succeeding one must not exit at all.

<<test functions>>=
def test_list_prints_every_bundled_tutorial(tmp_path, capsys):
  """tutorial list names each bundled tutorial in a tab-separated row"""
  args = build_parser(state_root=tmp_path).parse_args(["tutorial", "list"])

  tutorial.tutorial_command({}, None, args)

  lines = capsys.readouterr().out.splitlines()
  for path in bundled_tutorial_files():
    assert any(line.startswith(f"{path.stem}\t") for line in lines)

def test_a_failing_handler_exits_with_its_status():
  """A non-zero status from pytorial becomes the exit status"""
  args = argparse.Namespace(tutorial_handler=lambda args: 3)

  with pytest.raises(SystemExit) as excinfo:
    tutorial.tutorial_command({}, None, args)

  assert excinfo.value.code == 3

def test_a_succeeding_handler_returns_quietly():
  """A zero status from pytorial does not exit"""
  args = argparse.Namespace(tutorial_handler=lambda args: 0)

  assert tutorial.tutorial_command({}, None, args) is None
@

\section{What every bundled tutorial must satisfy}
\label{sec:tutorial-contract}

The tutorials are Markdown tangled from literate sources, and noweb
tangles whatever it is given: a chunk that is defined but never used is
left out of the output without a word, and a fence that is not closed
is only found when pytorial parses the file.
The build does not catch either, so the tests do.
Every bundled file must parse, must be named after its identifier so
that the file listing tells the user what to type after [[run]], and
must consist of steps that can actually be checked.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("path", bundled_tutorial_files(),
                         ids=lambda path: path.stem)
def test_bundled_tutorial_parses_and_is_named_after_its_id(path):
  """Each bundled tutorial loads and its file name is its identifier"""
  loaded = pytorial.catalog.load_tutorial_file(path)

  assert loaded.id == path.stem
  assert loaded.title and loaded.summary
  assert loaded.steps
@

A shell step without required patterns passes whatever the learner
does, and a question step without answers cannot be asked, so each step
must carry the check its kind needs.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("path", bundled_tutorial_files(),
                         ids=lambda path: path.stem)
def test_every_step_can_be_checked(path):
  """Shell steps have patterns, question steps have answers"""
  for step in pytorial.catalog.load_tutorial_file(path).steps:
    if step.kind is None:
      assert step.required_patterns, step.title
    else:
      assert step.answers, step.title
      if step.kind != "input":
        assert step.options, step.title
@

Finally, the promise from the trust model: no bundled step runs shell
code on the learner's behalf, so all of them work under
[[--no-allow-shell]].

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("path", bundled_tutorial_files(),
                         ids=lambda path: path.stem)
def test_no_bundled_step_uses_shell_hooks(path):
  """Bundled tutorials validate what was typed, never run their own shell"""
  for step in pytorial.catalog.load_tutorial_file(path).steps:
    assert step.pre_command is None, step.title
    assert step.check_command is None, step.title
    assert step.post_command is None, step.title
@

The tutorials also name commands and options of this program, and
those must exist; that check needs the whole parser tree and therefore
lives with the main program (\cref{sec:tutorials-name-real-commands}).
